<article class="atlas-note" aria-labelledby="atlas-note-story-title">
  <header class="atlas-note-header">
    <p class="atlas-note-kicker">Some stories take years to make sense</p>
    <h2 id="atlas-note-story-title" class="atlas-note-date">
      <time datetime="2021-12-12">12 / 12 / 2021</time>
    </h2>
  </header>

  <div class="atlas-note-body">
    <p>
      On
      <time class="atlas-note-highlight" datetime="2021-12-12"
        >December 12, 2021</time
      >, I was at the stadium for the final between Atlas and
      <span class="atlas-note-highlight">Le&oacute;n</span>. That day, Atlas
      became champion for the second time in its history, 70 years after its
      last title in <span class="atlas-note-highlight">1951</span>.
    </p>

    <p>It was one of the most memorable days of my life.</p>

    <p>But this story did not start that night.</p>

    <p>
      <time class="atlas-note-highlight" datetime="2001-10-13"
        >October 13, 2001</time
      >, the day when I watched my first match at the stadium. I had spent
      several weeks asking my father to take me, and when he finally did, the
      match was also against <span class="atlas-note-highlight"
        >Le&oacute;n</span
      >. <span class="atlas-note-highlight">Atlas won 4-1.</span> I do not remember
      the goals, but I do remember the atmosphere: all those people cheering for a
      team that many had already written off.
    </p>

    <p>That memory stayed with me.</p>

    <p>
      Yes, it is football. But for those who understand, it has never been just
      football. This was a team that, according to popular opinion, was doomed
      to mediocrity. A team many stopped believing in. A team that carried
      decades of frustration, heartbreak, and almosts.
    </p>

    <p>
      But no matter what, I always had hope. I always believed that one day this
      team would rise, and I followed them even through their worst seasons.
    </p>

    <p>
      Twenty years later, I was there again.
      <span class="atlas-note-highlight">Same rival.</span>
      <span class="atlas-note-highlight">Same stadium.</span> And in a way that made
      everything feel almost unreal, <span class="atlas-note-highlight"
        >the same side</span
      >: where also the final goal was scored.
    </p>

    <p>
      I never imagined that the first match I ever saw in person would be
      against the same team I would later see my team becoming champion. That
      made everything feel even more magical than it already was.
    </p>

    <p>And I was there.</p>

    <p>And yes, I cried.</p>

    <p>
      Maybe that is why I could never stop believing. Because with Atlas, hope
      has never been easy, but it has always been real.
    </p>

    <p>As we like to say:</p>
  </div>

